1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:06,985
Imagine asking one question, and instead of a single quick answer, a whole research team gets to work on it.

2
00:00:07,335 --> 00:00:13,714
They plan, search, read, check their own work, and hand you a report where every fact points to its source.

3
00:00:15,264 --> 00:00:22,389
Doing research by hand means twenty open tabs, copied notes, and losing track of where each fact came from.

4
00:00:22,739 --> 00:00:29,865
Asking a chatbot is fast, but it sounds just as confident when it has no evidence, and it can even make up a source.

5
00:00:30,215 --> 00:00:35,441
This agent sits in between: the speed of AI, with the habits of a careful researcher.

6
00:00:36,991 --> 00:00:40,618
Inside, there are eleven helpers, each with one small job.

7
00:00:40,968 --> 00:00:45,576
They don't chat with each other. They all read from and write into one shared notebook.

8
00:00:45,926 --> 00:00:50,854
And the notebook is saved after every step, so nothing is lost if something goes wrong.

9
00:00:52,404 --> 00:01:00,191
First, a doorman checks the question. Quick, free rules catch nonsense and tricks, like someone telling it to ignore its rules.

10
00:01:00,541 --> 00:01:08,989
Then a small, fast AI model decides if it's something you could really research. A request for a poem is politely turned away.

11
00:01:09,339 --> 00:01:15,931
If that model is down, the doorman lets the question through, because the checks at the end still protect the report.

12
00:01:17,481 --> 00:01:25,289
The lead reads your question and writes a short brief: the goal, the scope, and who the answer is for. It's told not to answer yet.

13
00:01:25,639 --> 00:01:30,993
The planner then breaks it into three to five smaller questions that can each be searched on their own.

14
00:01:31,343 --> 00:01:38,959
At the same time, the librarian checks your past research. If you've asked something similar before, those findings are reused.

15
00:01:40,509 --> 00:01:45,821
A good question isn't a good search. So each small question is turned into three searches.

16
00:01:46,171 --> 00:01:53,126
Keywords for the web, exact phrases for your own documents, and a short topic for Wikipedia and research papers.

17
00:01:54,676 --> 00:02:02,740
All the searches run at the same time, in three places: the web, documents you uploaded, and Wikipedia and research papers.

18
00:02:03,090 --> 00:02:09,170
The web has backups. If one search service fails or finds nothing, the next one in line takes over.

19
00:02:09,520 --> 00:02:17,861
A failure is written in the notebook as a note, never a crash. And repeated searches come from a twenty-four hour store, so they cost nothing.

20
00:02:19,411 --> 00:02:27,689
A reader goes through every result, pulls out the sentences that actually answer the question, and gives each one a score from zero to ten.

21
00:02:28,039 --> 00:02:29,916
Anything under six is thrown away.

22
00:02:30,266 --> 00:02:38,330
If two websites copied the same press release, only the better copy is kept. And the notebook holds at most forty pieces of evidence.

23
00:02:39,880 --> 00:02:43,997
Next, a checker asks: is every small question actually answered?

24
00:02:44,347 --> 00:02:50,001
If something is missing, it says exactly what, and the team searches again, only for the gaps.

25
00:02:50,351 --> 00:02:57,903
But it can loop at most two extra times. After that, it writes the report anyway, and says honestly what it couldn't find.

26
00:02:59,453 --> 00:03:03,826
Now a writer drafts the report, using only the evidence in the notebook.

27
00:03:04,176 --> 00:03:10,533
Every sentence ends with a number that points to a piece of evidence. Where sources disagree, it says so.

28
00:03:12,083 --> 00:03:19,529
An editor scores the draft from one to five on three things: is it clear, is every claim backed up, and is anything missing.

29
00:03:19,879 --> 00:03:25,916
All three need a four or better. If not, the editor writes specific notes, and the writer rewrites.

30
00:03:26,266 --> 00:03:30,127
Again, at most two rewrites, so a fussy editor can't loop forever.

31
00:03:31,677 --> 00:03:38,099
Last, a citation checker goes sentence by sentence and fixes any number that points to the wrong evidence.

32
00:03:38,449 --> 00:03:45,595
Then plain code, not AI, removes any number that points to nothing, like a ninety-nine when there are only thirty.

33
00:03:45,945 --> 00:03:53,711
The list of sources is also built by code from the evidence itself. So the AI never gets the chance to invent a link.

34
00:03:55,261 --> 00:03:59,890
What you get is a report with a summary, key findings, and a numbered source list.

35
00:04:00,240 --> 00:04:05,552
At the bottom, research notes list anything it couldn't back up, and any limit it hit along the way.

36
00:04:07,102 --> 00:04:12,478
Each finished report is saved, so your next related question starts from what you already know.

37
00:04:12,828 --> 00:04:17,287
And while it works, you watch each helper light up on screen, one step at a time.

38
00:04:17,637 --> 00:04:24,954
The whole team is also tested without the internet or any paid AI, using a stand-in model that follows a script.

39
00:04:26,504 --> 00:04:28,275
Plan. Search. Check. Cite.

40
00:04:28,625 --> 00:04:31,099
It doesn't just answer. It shows its work.
